    \u0442\u0440\u0443\u0434\u0430\u043c, \u043f\u0435\u0440\u0435\u0436\u0438\u0432\u0448\u0438\u043c
    \u0432\u0435\u043a\u0430."
  shortBioEn: "The Diofant of Alexandria (C. III of the century C.C.) is one of the greatest mathematicians of antiquity whose ideas have been ahead of their time for centuries.He is often called the \"father of algebra\"; he is first known as the author of the fundamental work \"Arifmetics\"./n/n Despite the great influence of his work, the very life of Diofant is very little known; she is surrounded by secrets and speculations that still cause controversy among historians of science./n/nK when and where did Diofant live?/n/n is known only that Diofant lived and worked in Alexandria of Egypt.It was the largest scientific center in the ellinistic world, where the famous Alexandria Library was located, and this place of work is listed in all sources./n/Nactical dates of birth and death of Diofant are the subject of many years of discussion.History can only establish a \"fork\" at about 500 years of age:/n/n Lower border: in the tract \"On Multiangular Numbers\", Diofant refers to Greek mathematician Hypsicles living in the middle of the second century BCE, so Diofant lived later./n Upper border: Mathematician Theon of Alexandre (Father of Hypathia), living in the mid-fourth century CE, quotes from \"Arifmetics\".So, Diofant lived before. /n/n Long time most historians attributed the life of Diofant to the middle of the third century C.E. (c. 250) This conclusion was based on the assumption that the Honourable Dionisius mentioned in the dedication to Arifmetics was Bishop Dionisius of Alexandria, who lived at that time./n/nnnn However, modern research, in particular the work of W.Knorra questions this date: there is reason to believe that Diofant could have lived earlier, perhaps even in the first century CE The spread of dates in encyclopedia is very large, from c. 200 to 409.The CE/n/nEpitaphy of Diofant/n/N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-N-E-N-N-NHowever, she gives the following figures: /n/n \"The Tomb of Diofant; be surprised at it, and the stone of wisdom will speak to him by the dead age; the will of the gods he lived a sixth of his life; and the sixth half met with a gun on his cheeks; only passed the seventh, he was engaged to his friend; with her, five years after having spent, the wise man waited for his son; only his beloved son lived half his life; he was taken from his father by his early grave.Twice for two years, the parent mourned the bitter grief, and saw the limit of his sad life.\" /n/n If this equation is resolved, it turns out that Diofant lived 84 years.From the epithephia, you can \"know\" the timeline of his life:/n/n/n Childhood: up to 14 years of age (1/6 of 84)./n Youth (beard formation): up to 21 years of age (a further 1/12 of life)./n Married: 33 years of age (another 1/7 of life, i.e. + 12 years)./n Son's birth: 38 years of age (five years after marriage)./n Son's death: son lived half of the father's life - 42 years of age and died when Diofant was 80 years of age./n Death of Diofant: 4 years after the death of his son, 84 years of age./n/n Scientific heritage/n/Nnamely labour,It's not a biography, it's made Diofant immortal. /n/n \"Arifmetics\"/n/n This is not an arithmetic textbook in the modern sense, but a compilation of about 260 tasks with solutions./n/n Structure: originally, the work consisted of 13 books.Not all of us have learned: 6 books were preserved in Greek, and 4 (books IV - VII) were found in Arabic in the Mesheda Library (Iran) in 1968./n Main idea: The Diofant sought positive rational solutions (intellectual and fractional) for uncertain equations.Unlike many late mathematicians, he was interested not only in whole numbers: he was the first in antiquity to treat fractions as natural numbers. /n/n/n Revolution in symbol/n/nDiofant made a breakthrough by introducing a developed literal symbol. Before it, mathematical expressions were described in words. He began to use abbreviations for the unknown, his square, cube and other degrees, as well as for the act of subtraction.It made the notes compact and brought the algebra closer to what we know now./n/nother works/n/n from other compositions of it were left with only fragments:/n/n \"On multi-angular numbers\" — tract,Pythagore's continuing ideas./n \"Prizma\" is known only for the quotes in Arifmetics./n/n The influence on world science/n/n Arab world: in the Xth century, \"Arifmetika\" was translated into Arabic and influenced the mathematicians of the Islamic world./n Europe of New Time: in 1575, the first Latin translation appeared, and in 1621, the classic translation of Bache de Meziriac.It was on the fields of this copy that Pierre Ferma recorded his famous Great Theorem, claiming that he had found \"a truly remarkable proof.\"/n Name in centuries: in honor of Diofant, an entire section of mathematics was named, the diofents of the equation (the equations in whole numbers).His ideas formed the basis for the work of the European Commission, the European Commission, the European Commission, the European Commission, the European Commission, the European Union, the European Union, the European Union, the European Union, the European Union and the European Union. His ideas were based on the work of the European Commission, the European Commission, the European Commission, the European Union, the European Union, the European Union, the European Union, the European Union, the European Union, the European Union, the European Union and the European Union.The main source is epitaphia-task./n Main work: \"Arifmetica\" (13 books, preservation 10) is a set of problems for solving equations./n Main discovery: the introduction of literal symbols and the consideration of fractions as numbers./n* Contribution to science: one of the founders of the algebra (as well as Al-Khorezi); the name given to the whole class of equations./n/n/n Life of the Diofant, wrapped in a secret, makes its figure even more magnificent: we know it from the brilliant work that has survived centuries."
  interestingFactsRu: "\u041d\u0435\u0441\u043c\u043e\u0442\u0440\u044f \u043d\u0430 \u0442\u043e, \u0447\u0442\u043e
